## 2. Fraud detection

**Requirement:** decide within 200ms of the transaction, using rules the fraud team can
change without a deploy.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    P[Payments service] --> K[(Kafka: transactions)]
    R[(Kafka: rules<br/>compacted)] -->|broadcast| F
    K --> F["Flink<br/>KeyedBroadcastProcessFunction"]
    F --> A[(Kafka: alerts)]
    F -->|Async I/O| DB[(Customer profile store)]
    A --> S[Decision service]
```

| Dimension | Decision |
| --- | --- |
| **Time** | Event time for the pattern logic; a tight bound (1–2s) because latency is the requirement. Processing-time timers only for liveness. |
| **State** | Per-account rolling window, bounded by the widest rule. **Cap the rule window in validation** — see [Project 4](/docs/flink/projects/dynamic-rules). |
| **Checkpoints** | Frequent (10–30s), unaligned enabled. State is small; recovery speed matters more than checkpoint cost. |
| **Sinks** | Idempotent, keyed on `(account, triggering_txn_id)`. **Not** 2PC — it would add checkpoint-interval latency to a 200ms budget. |
| **Failure** | Replay re-emits alerts; the idempotent key absorbs it. The decision service must be idempotent too. |
| **Scaling** | Keyed by account — naturally well distributed. Watch for merchant-level skew if you key on merchant instead. |
| **Observe** | Alert rate, rule count (alert if it drops to zero), p99 end-to-end latency, expired-pattern rate. |

<Callout type="key">

The 200ms requirement is what rules out two-phase commit. With a 2PC sink, results are
invisible until the covering checkpoint completes — so a 10s checkpoint interval means a
10s latency floor.

Idempotent output plus at-least-once delivery is the correct choice here, and being able
to explain *why* is the difference between configuring a system and designing one.

</Callout>

