## 3. Sessionization

**Requirement:** user sessions with a 30-minute gap, landed in the lakehouse for
analysis, plus live session counts.

| Dimension | Decision |
| --- | --- |
| **Time** | Event time, mandatory. A gap is a real-world quantity; processing time would make session boundaries depend on your lag. |
| **State** | The largest of these six. One open session per active user; state lives as long as the user is active. Size it from peak concurrent users, not total users. |
| **Checkpoints** | Long interval (2–5 min), incremental, RocksDB on local NVMe. State size dominates duration. |
| **Sinks** | Append-only to Iceberg — a session emits once, when it closes. Live counts via a separate short-window aggregation. |
| **Failure** | Restore is slow because state is large. **Enable local recovery** — this is the single biggest lever on recovery time here. |
| **Scaling** | Keyed by user; well distributed. Parallelism driven by state size per subtask, not by CPU. |
| **Observe** | Open session count, state size per subtask, restore duration. |

<Callout type="prod">

Sessionization is where **restore time** becomes your real SLA rather than checkpoint
time. Measure it explicitly: take a savepoint, restart, and time how long until the first
checkpoint succeeds after the restore.

If that number is unacceptable, local recovery and a warm node pool will help far more
than any checkpoint tuning.

</Callout>

