## 4. CDC to lakehouse

**Requirement:** replicate operational tables into a queryable lake, with deletes and
updates applied correctly.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    PG[(Postgres)] -->|Debezium| K[(Kafka<br/>debezium-json)]
    K --> F["Flink SQL<br/>INSERT INTO … SELECT"]
    F --> I[(Iceberg, upsert enabled)]
    I --> T[Trino / Athena / Spark]
    C["Compaction job<br/>scheduled, separate"] -.-> I
```

| Dimension | Decision |
| --- | --- |
| **Time** | Mostly processing time — CDC ordering comes from the log, not from event time. Use event time if you need temporal joins against the CDC stream. |
| **State** | Small if it is a pure passthrough. `ChangelogNormalize` adds per-key state if the source is an upsert connector — check the plan. |
| **Checkpoints** | Drives commit frequency to Iceberg. 1–5 minutes: shorter means more small files, longer means staler data. |
| **Sinks** | Iceberg with `write.upsert.enabled`, committing on checkpoint. Exactly-once via the same 2PC protocol. |
| **Failure** | Replay re-applies the same upserts — idempotent by primary key. |
| **Scaling** | Bounded by the source database's change rate. Rarely a Flink-side problem. |
| **Observe** | Replication lag, commit frequency, **file count per partition**. |

<Callout type="mistake" title="The small-file problem is not optional">

A streaming CDC sink writes files on every checkpoint, per partition, per subtask. At a
1-minute interval with 8 subtasks and 10 partitions, that is 4,800 files a day. Query
performance degrades steadily and then sharply.

**Compaction is a separate scheduled job, and every deployment needs one.** Forgetting it
is the most common reason these pipelines are fine in month one and unusable in month
six.

</Callout>

