## 5. Real-time feature generation for ML

**Requirement:** compute features for online inference, with identical logic for training
and serving.

| Dimension | Decision |
| --- | --- |
| **Time** | Event time, strictly. Training/serving skew is *caused* by processing-time features. |
| **State** | Windowed aggregates per entity. Multiple window sizes — 1 min, 1 hour, 1 day — so the state multiplier matters. |
| **Checkpoints** | Standard, 60s. |
| **Sinks** | A low-latency store (Redis, DynamoDB) for serving, plus a lakehouse table for training. **Same job writes both** — that is what eliminates skew. |
| **Failure** | Idempotent upserts keyed on `(entity, feature, window)`. |
| **Scaling** | Keyed by entity. Watch for skew on popular entities. |
| **Observe** | Feature freshness, null rate, distribution drift. |

<Callout type="key">

The entire value of streaming feature generation is that **one job produces both the
training features and the serving features**.

The moment training features are computed by a separate batch job, you have
training/serving skew, and your model's offline metrics stop predicting its online
behaviour. That is a Lambda problem wearing an ML hat.

</Callout>

