## 6. IoT / device telemetry

**Requirement:** ingest millions of devices, detect anomalies, handle devices that go
offline for days.

| Dimension | Decision |
| --- | --- |
| **Time** | Event time with a **wide** bound (minutes) — devices buffer while offline. Clamp future timestamps aggressively; device clocks are the worst clocks. |
| **State** | Per-device state with an aggressive TTL. Key cardinality is the dominant cost — millions of devices means millions of state entries. |
| **Checkpoints** | Long interval, incremental, RocksDB. Move the **timer service to RocksDB** — millions of device timers will OOM the heap otherwise. |
| **Sinks** | Time-series store for telemetry, alerts topic for anomalies. |
| **Failure** | Standard. Devices resend on reconnect, so duplicates are common — dedup on `(device, sequence)`. |
| **Scaling** | Keyed by device; excellent distribution. Bounded by source partitions. |
| **Observe** | Per-device lateness distribution, active device count, TTL eviction rate. |

<Callout type="prod" title="Two settings specific to this shape">

```yaml
state.backend.rocksdb.timer-service.factory: rocksdb
```

Because with one timer per device and millions of devices, the default heap timer service
is the thing that kills you — not the state.

And a **generous `withIdleness`**, because devices legitimately go quiet for hours. Set it
longer than your longest normal silence, or you will mark active devices idle and
classify their catch-up data as late.

</Callout>

